# Zenith Trainer - Cursor AI Rules
# Fitness tracking application with AI-powered insights
# Stack: Next.js 15 + TypeScript + Firebase + Genkit AI + Radix UI + Tailwind CSS

## Project Overview
You are working on Zenith Trainer, a comprehensive fitness and habit tracking application with AI-powered insights, workout progression, and program management.

## Core Technologies
- **Framework**: Next.js 15.5.6 (App Router, React Server Components)
- **Language**: TypeScript 5 (strict mode enabled)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS 3.4 + tailwind-merge + class-variance-authority
- **UI Components**: Radix UI primitives + custom shadcn/ui components
- **Backend**: Firebase 11.9 (Auth, Firestore, Storage, Admin SDK)
- **AI**: Google Genkit 1.20 with Google Generative AI
- **Forms**: React Hook Form 7.54 + Zod 3.24 validation
- **State**: React hooks, Firebase real-time subscriptions
- **Testing**: Playwright (E2E), TypeScript type checking
- **Package Manager**: npm

## Code Style & Patterns

### TypeScript
- **ALWAYS use strict TypeScript** - no `any` types unless absolutely necessary
- Use explicit return types for functions
- Prefer `type` over `interface` for consistency
- Use Zod schemas for runtime validation
- Example:
  ```typescript
  type User = {
    id: string;
    name: string;
  };

  const getUser = (id: string): Promise<User> => {...}
  ```

### React & Next.js
- **Prefer React Server Components (RSC)** by default
- Use `'use client'` directive ONLY when:
  - Using React hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.)
  - Handling browser events
  - Using context providers
- Follow Next.js App Router conventions:
  - `page.tsx` for routes
  - `layout.tsx` for shared layouts
  - `route.ts` for API routes
  - `loading.tsx` for loading states
- Use Next.js built-in features:
  - `next/link` for navigation
  - `next/image` for images
  - Dynamic imports for code splitting
- Minimize `useEffect` usage - prefer RSC data fetching
- Use async/await in Server Components for data fetching

### Component Patterns
- **Functional components only** - no class components
- Use composition over prop drilling
- Keep components small and focused (< 200 lines)
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Props interface should be defined inline or as type above component
- Example structure:
  ```typescript
  'use client';

  import { useState } from 'react';
  import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';

  interface MyComponentProps {
    title: string;
    onAction: () => void;
  }

  export function MyComponent({ title, onAction }: MyComponentProps) {
    const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);

    return (
      <div>
        <h2>{title}</h2>
        <Button onClick={onAction}>Action</Button>
      </div>
    );
  }
  ```

### Firebase
- **ALWAYS use Firebase hooks** from `/src/firebase/provider.tsx`:
  - `useUser()` for authentication
  - `useFirestore()` for Firestore instance
  - `useCollection()` for real-time collections
  - `useDoc()` for real-time documents
- Handle Firebase errors properly using error emitter
- Use `WithId<T>` type for documents with Firestore IDs
- Structure Firestore paths: `users/{userId}/{collection}`
- Use Firebase Admin SDK only in API routes (server-side)
- Example:
  ```typescript
  const { user } = useUser();
  const firestore = useFirestore();

  const workoutsQuery = useMemoFirebase(
    () => user ? collection(firestore, `users/${user.uid}/workouts`) : null,
    [user, firestore]
  );

  const { data: workouts } = useCollection<Workout>(workoutsQuery);
  ```

### AI & Genkit
- Use Genkit flows for AI operations (in `/src/ai/flows/`)
- Always handle AI errors gracefully with try-catch
- Use `@ts-expect-error - Genkit type wrapper issue` for known Genkit type issues
- Structure AI prompts with clear input/output schemas
- Log AI operations with descriptive messages
- Example:
  ```typescript
  try {
    const result = await aiFlow({ input: data });
    // @ts-expect-error - Genkit type wrapper issue
    return result.output ?? result;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('[ai-operation] Error:', error);
    throw error;
  }
  ```

### Styling with Tailwind
- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling
- Use `cn()` helper from `@/lib/utils` to merge classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design
- Use CSS variables for theme colors (defined in globals.css)
- Prefer Tailwind over inline styles or CSS modules
- Use `class-variance-authority` for component variants
- Example:
  ```typescript
  import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';

  <div className={cn(
    "flex items-center gap-4",
    isActive && "bg-primary text-primary-foreground",
    className
  )}>
  ```

### UI Components (Radix UI + shadcn/ui)
- Use existing UI components from `/src/components/ui/`
- Don't recreate components that already exist
- Follow Radix UI patterns for accessibility
- Use compound components pattern
- Available components:
  - Button, Input, Textarea, Select, Checkbox, Switch
  - Dialog, Alert Dialog, Popover, Tooltip, Toast
  - Card, Tabs, Accordion, Separator, Skeleton
  - Form components with React Hook Form integration

### Forms & Validation
- Use React Hook Form for form state management
- Define Zod schemas for validation
- Use `@hookform/resolvers/zod` for integration
- Show validation errors inline
- Example:
  ```typescript
  const schema = z.object({
    name: z.string().min(1, 'Name is required'),
    email: z.string().email('Invalid email'),
  });

  type FormValues = z.infer<typeof schema>;

  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm<FormValues>({
    resolver: zodResolver(schema),
  });
  ```

### Error Handling
- Use try-catch for async operations
- **NEVER use empty catch blocks** - always log errors
- Use custom error types from `/src/firebase/errors.ts`
- Use error emitter for Firebase permission errors
- Show user-friendly error messages with Toast
- Example:
  ```typescript
  try {
    await operation();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Operation failed:', error);
    toast({
      title: 'Error',
      description: 'Something went wrong. Please try again.',
      variant: 'destructive',
    });
  }
  ```

### File Organization
- Group related components in folders
- Use barrel exports (index.ts) sparingly
- Keep utilities in `/src/lib/`
- Keep types in `/src/lib/types.ts` or colocated with components
- Keep AI flows in `/src/ai/flows/`
- Keep Firebase hooks in `/src/firebase/`
- Structure:
  ```
  src/
  ├── app/                 # Next.js App Router pages
  ├── components/          # React components
  │   ├── ui/             # Base UI components (shadcn)
  │   └── [feature]/      # Feature-specific components
  ├── lib/                # Utilities and helpers
  ├── firebase/           # Firebase configuration and hooks
  ├── ai/                 # Genkit AI flows and prompts
  └── hooks/              # Custom React hooks
  ```

## Naming Conventions
- **Files**: kebab-case (e.g., `workout-builder.tsx`)
- **Components**: PascalCase (e.g., `WorkoutBuilder`)
- **Functions/Variables**: camelCase (e.g., `getUserWorkouts`)
- **Types/Interfaces**: PascalCase (e.g., `WorkoutExtended`)
- **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_WORKOUTS`)
- **Hooks**: camelCase with 'use' prefix (e.g., `useWorkouts`)

## Import Order
1. React and Next.js imports
2. External library imports (alphabetically)
3. Internal components (`@/components`)
4. Internal utilities (`@/lib`)
5. Types
6. Styles

Example:
```typescript
'use client';

import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';

import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';
import { Dialog } from '@/components/ui/dialog';

import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';
import type { Workout } from '@/lib/types';
```

## Testing
- Write E2E tests with Playwright in `/e2e/`
- Use descriptive test names
- Test user flows, not implementation details
- Use `playwright.config.ts` configuration
- Run tests with `npm run test:e2e`

## Performance
- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components
- Use `useMemo()` and `useCallback()` appropriately
- Lazy load heavy components with `next/dynamic`
- Optimize images with `next/image`
- Minimize client-side JavaScript
- Use RSC for data fetching when possible

## Security
- **NEVER commit sensitive data** (API keys, credentials)
- Store secrets in environment variables
- Use Firebase Security Rules for access control
- Validate all user inputs with Zod
- Sanitize data before rendering
- Use Firebase Admin SDK only in API routes

## Environment Variables
- Store in `.env.local` (never commit this file)
- Use `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix for client-side variables
- Reference `.env.example` for required variables
- Required variables:
  - Firebase config (NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_*)
  - Google AI API key (GOOGLE_GENAI_API_KEY)

## Git Practices
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Use conventional commits format:
  - `feat:` for new features
  - `fix:` for bug fixes
  - `docs:` for documentation
  - `refactor:` for code refactoring
  - `test:` for tests
  - `chore:` for maintenance
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Example: `feat: add workout progression suggestions with AI`

## Documentation
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Keep README.md up to date
- Document non-obvious code decisions
- Use TODO comments sparingly and track them
- Format:
  ```typescript
  /**
   * Calculates workout progression based on history
   * @param workoutId - The workout to analyze
   * @param days - Number of days to look back
   * @returns Progression suggestions
   */
  ```

## Debugging
- Use proper logging system (see `/src/lib/logger.ts` - TODO)
- Use descriptive log messages
- Include context in error logs
- Use browser DevTools for client debugging
- Use Next.js built-in error handling

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid
- ❌ Don't use `any` type - use `unknown` or proper types
- ❌ Don't use `console.log` in production - use logger utility
- ❌ Don't fetch data in `useEffect` - use RSC or React Query
- ❌ Don't mutate state directly - use setState or reducer
- ❌ Don't forget error boundaries for error handling
- ❌ Don't use `var` - always use `const` or `let`
- ❌ Don't ignore TypeScript errors - fix them properly
- ❌ Don't use deprecated APIs - check documentation

## AI Assistance Guidelines
When I ask you to:
- **"Create a component"** → Follow component patterns above
- **"Add a feature"** → Update related tests and types
- **"Fix a bug"** → Identify root cause, add tests, fix properly
- **"Refactor code"** → Maintain functionality, improve structure
- **"Add AI feature"** → Use Genkit flows, handle errors
- **"Add Firebase feature"** → Use hooks, handle permissions

## When in Doubt
1. Check existing similar code in the project
2. Refer to official documentation (Next.js, React, Firebase)
3. Follow TypeScript strict mode guidelines
4. Ask clarifying questions before making assumptions
5. Prefer simplicity over cleverness

## Project-Specific Notes
- This is a fitness tracking app with workouts, habits, and programs
- AI features use Google Gemini via Genkit
- Real-time updates via Firebase subscriptions
- Focus on user experience and performance
- Accessibility is important (use Radix UI patterns)

